# The live-database demo

The live-database demo turns grobase from an abstract backend into something you
can *see*: a deterministic dataset, seeded through a real API key, that osionos
reads and writes as a genuine live database.

## One command

```bash
make seed-live-demo
```

From the root this is a thin delegator — it runs `make -C apps/grobase
seed-live-demo`, then `make osionos-app-live` to rebuild osionos-app with the
BaaS environment baked in.

## What gets seeded

> [!info] Deterministic by design
> The generator produces a **~164k-row commerce + ops dataset** with a fixed PRNG
> seed (**42**). Same seed, same rows, every run — so the demo is reproducible
> across machines.

- **Idempotent** — re-running converges; it doesn't duplicate.
- **`RESEED=1`** wipes first for a clean regenerate.
- Every row is **owner-stamped with the app key's principal**, so the demo data
  respects the same per-request owner-scoping as real tenant data.

The generator lives at `apps/grobase/scripts/seed/`, alongside the other tenant
seeders (agency, gourmand, red-tetris).

## The key that makes it live

The demo runs against a **provisioned `mbk_` app key**, surfaced to osionos as
`OSIONOS_BAAS_API_KEY`:

| Environment mode | What the demo does |
|:-----------------|:-------------------|
| With the app key set | Full live-DB demo — osionos reads/writes real grobase tables |
| Without it | Core login/editing still works; the live-DB demo stays **dormant** |

> [!note] No vault key required for the basics
> When `make all` comes up with no vault access, core login and editing work
> immediately. The live-DB demo (and the agency permissions) stay dormant until
> their own seed steps run — they need the provisioned app key, not just any
> secret.

## How osionos reaches it

osionos consolidated on the single grobase/Kong backend — `VITE_BAAS_URL` points
at Kong (`http://127.0.0.1:8000` for desktop, which must use `127.0.0.1` since
Kong is IPv4-only). Reads and writes flow through the same
[Kong](kong-api-gateway.xmd) routes as any other client.

> [!tip] Want the data portable instead of regenerated?
> If you'd rather restore a captured dataset than regenerate one, see the
> [demo-data snapshots toolkit](data-snapshots.xmd).
